'use strict';
// App-authored concept checks and formula summaries, organized by the uploaded textbooks' chapters.
window.STUDY_DATA={calculus:[
[1,'Functions and models','For a function f, what must be true about each input in its domain?',['It has exactly one output.','It has exactly two outputs.','It must be positive.','It must be an integer.'],0,'A function assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain. Different inputs may share an output.','(f ∘ g)(x) = f(g(x))','Composition is defined where x is in the domain of g and g(x) is in the domain of f.'],
[2,'Limits and derivatives','What does f′(a) represent geometrically?',['The area under f up to a.','The slope of the tangent at x = a.','The value f(a).','The average value of f.'],1,'The derivative is the limit of secant slopes, so it gives the tangent slope when that limit exists.','f′(a) = limₕ→₀ [f(a + h) − f(a)]/h','The two-sided limit must exist and be finite for the ordinary derivative.'],
[3,'Differentiation rules','For differentiable u and v, which is the derivative of uv?',['u′v′','u′ + v′','u′v + uv′','uv'],2,'The product rule differentiates one factor at a time and adds the two resulting terms.','(uv)′ = u′v + uv′; (f(g(x)))′ = f′(g(x))g′(x)','Use the product rule for products and the chain rule for nested functions.'],
[4,'Applications of derivatives','If f′ is positive throughout an interval, what follows?',['f is increasing there.','f is necessarily positive there.','f is decreasing there.','f is constant there.'],0,'A positive derivative means the output increases as the input increases. It does not determine the sign of f itself.','f′ > 0 ⇒ increasing; f″ > 0 ⇒ concave up','Apply on an interval where the stated derivatives exist.'],
[5,'Integrals','What does the definite integral of velocity over time give?',['Final speed.','Total distance in every case.','Displacement.','Acceleration.'],2,'Integrating signed velocity gives displacement. Total distance instead integrates the absolute value of velocity.','∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a), where F′ = f','For the standard Fundamental Theorem, take f continuous on [a,b].'],
[6,'Applications of integration','Which integral gives area between an upper curve u and lower curve l on [a,b]?',['∫ₐᵇ (u + l) dx','∫ₐᵇ (u − l) dx','∫ₐᵇ ul dx','u(b) − l(a)'],1,'Each vertical strip has height upper minus lower. Integrating those strip heights gives the area.','A = ∫ₐᵇ [u(x) − l(x)] dx','Require u ≥ l; split at intersections if the order changes.'],
[7,'Integration techniques','Integration by parts reverses which differentiation rule?',['Power rule only.','Quotient rule.','Product rule.','Chain rule only.'],2,'Integrating the product rule d(uv) = u dv + v du gives the integration-by-parts identity.','∫u dv = uv − ∫v du','Choose u and dv so the remaining integral is simpler; include the integration constant.'],
[8,'Further integral applications','For a nonnegative probability density p on its full domain, what must its integral equal?',['0','1','The largest value of p.','The domain length.'],1,'The total probability over all possible values is one. The density itself can exceed one over a short interval.','L = ∫ₐᵇ √(1 + [f′(x)]²) dx','Arc length for a continuously differentiable graph y = f(x) on [a,b].'],
[9,'Differential equations','In Euler’s method, which slope advances the current point?',['The slope at the next, unknown point.','The average of all prior slopes.','The current slope f(xₙ,yₙ).','The slope zero in every step.'],2,'Forward Euler uses the known current slope and step size h to estimate the next value.','yₙ₊₁ = yₙ + h f(xₙ,yₙ); xₙ₊₁ = xₙ + h','Approximation for y′ = f(x,y); smaller h often improves accuracy but stability still matters.'],
[10,'Parametric and polar curves','For x = x(t), y = y(t), how is dy/dx computed when x′(t) ≠ 0?',['y′(t)/x′(t)','x′(t)/y′(t)','x′(t)y′(t)','y(t)/x(t)'],0,'Both derivatives are with respect to t. Dividing eliminates the common parameter change.','dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt); A = ½∫αᵝ r(θ)² dθ','The slope formula requires dx/dt ≠ 0. The polar area formula assumes the region is swept once.'],
[11,'Sequences and series','If aₙ does not approach zero, what can you conclude about Σaₙ?',['It converges absolutely.','It diverges.','It converges conditionally.','Its sum is zero.'],1,'A necessary condition for convergence is aₙ → 0. That condition alone is not sufficient.','Σₙ₌₀∞ arⁿ = a/(1 − r), for |r| < 1','The geometric-series sum applies only when the common ratio has magnitude below one.'],
[12,'Vectors in space','What is the dot product of two nonzero perpendicular vectors?',['The product of their lengths.','One.','Zero.','The sum of their lengths.'],2,'The dot product is |a||b| cos θ, and cos 90° = 0.','a · b = |a||b| cos θ; |a × b| = |a||b| sin θ','Dot product is scalar; cross product in three dimensions is a vector oriented by the right-hand rule.'],
[13,'Vector functions','If r(t) is position, which vector gives acceleration?',['r(t)','r′(t)','r″(t)','r(t) × r′(t)'],2,'Velocity is the first time derivative of position. Acceleration is the derivative of velocity.','v = r′(t); a = r″(t); speed = |r′(t)|','Speed is scalar; velocity and acceleration are vectors.'],
[14,'Partial derivatives','When finding ∂f/∂x for f(x,y), how is y treated?',['As a constant.','As equal to x.','As zero.','As a function of x in every case.'],0,'A partial derivative varies one independent variable while holding the others fixed.','Dᵤf = ∇f · u, where |u| = 1','The directional vector must be a unit vector and f must be differentiable at the point.'],
[15,'Multiple integrals','Which area element is used in polar coordinates?',['dr dθ','r dr dθ','r² dr dθ','sin θ dr dθ'],1,'The Jacobian factor r accounts for the growing arc length of an angular strip as radius increases.','dA = r dr dθ; dV = ρ² sin φ dρ dφ dθ','For spherical coordinates here, φ is measured from the positive z-axis.'],
[16,'Vector calculus','For a conservative field F = ∇f, what determines the line integral from A to B?',['Only the curve length.','Only the travel time.','f(B) − f(A).','The maximum magnitude of F.'],2,'The fundamental theorem for line integrals makes the integral depend only on the endpoint potential difference.','∫C ∇f · dr = f(B) − f(A)','Assume f has continuous first partial derivatives along a piecewise smooth path in its domain.'],
[17,'Second-order differential equations','For y″ + ω²y = 0 with ω > 0, which family is the general solution?',['C₁eᵡ + C₂e⁻ˣ for every ω.','C₁ cos(ωx) + C₂ sin(ωx).','C₁ + C₂x.','C₁x² + C₂.'],1,'Differentiating sine or cosine twice multiplies it by −ω², which makes y″ + ω²y equal zero.','ay″ + by′ + cy = 0 ⇒ ar² + br + c = 0','For constant coefficients and a ≠ 0; repeated and complex characteristic roots require their corresponding solution forms.']
],physics:[
[1,'Measurement','When adding measured lengths, what must you do first?',['Multiply their units.','Express them in compatible units.','Ignore their units.','Round them all to integers.'],1,'Addition requires the same physical dimension and compatible units. Convert before combining values.','1 km = 10³ m; 1 h = 3600 s','Track units through each conversion; dimensionless conversion ratios equal one.'],
[2,'Motion in one dimension','Can an object have zero velocity at an instant and nonzero acceleration?',['Yes, such as at the top of a vertical toss.','No, acceleration must then be zero.','Only if its mass is zero.','Only if no forces act.'],0,'At the top of an ideal vertical toss, instantaneous velocity is zero while gravitational acceleration remains downward.','v = v₀ + at; x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at²','These kinematic equations require constant acceleration.'],
[3,'Vectors','For a vector at angle θ from +x, what is its x component?',['A sin θ','A tan θ','A cos θ','A/θ'],2,'The adjacent projection on the x-axis is magnitude times cosine of the angle measured from +x.','Aₓ = A cos θ; Aᵧ = A sin θ','Use the stated angle convention and retain the signs of the components.'],
[4,'Motion in two and three dimensions','Without air resistance, what is a projectile’s horizontal acceleration?',['g','−g','Its launch speed.','Zero.'],3,'In the ideal projectile model gravity acts vertically, so horizontal velocity remains constant.','x = x₀ + v₀ₓt; y = y₀ + v₀ᵧt − ½gt²','Flat-Earth model, constant g, upward +y, and no air resistance.'],
[5,'Force and motion I','An object moves at constant velocity. What is its net force in an inertial frame?',['Zero.','Equal to its momentum.','Always its weight.','Increasing with time.'],0,'Constant velocity means zero acceleration. Newton’s second law then gives zero net force.','ΣF = ma','Constant-mass particle in an inertial frame; apply the vector equation component by component.'],
[6,'Force and motion II','Which statement about static friction is accurate?',['It always equals μₛN.','It adjusts up to a maximum μₛN.','It always points in the direction of motion.','It is independent of contact forces.'],1,'Static friction adjusts to prevent slipping until the required magnitude exceeds μₛN.','|fₛ| ≤ μₛN; fₖ = μₖN; aᵣ = v²/r','The kinetic-friction relation is the ideal sliding model. Centripetal acceleration points toward the curvature center.'],
[7,'Kinetic energy and work','A force always perpendicular to an object’s displacement does how much work?',['Positive work.','Negative work.','Zero work.','Work equal to its magnitude.'],2,'The work element is F · dr. Perpendicular vectors have zero dot product.','Wnet = ΔK; K = ½mv²','Classical particle kinetic energy; net work includes all forces.'],
[8,'Potential energy and conservation','When only conservative forces do work, which quantity is constant?',['Kinetic energy alone.','Potential energy alone.','Kinetic plus potential energy.','Speed in every situation.'],2,'Conservative forces exchange kinetic and potential energy while their sum remains constant.','Kᵢ + Uᵢ = Kf + Uf','With nonconservative work, use Δ(K + U) = Wnonconservative.'],
[9,'Center of mass and momentum','When is the total momentum of a system conserved?',['When every object stops.','When net external force is zero.','Only for elastic collisions.','Whenever kinetic energy decreases.'],1,'Internal forces cancel in the system momentum balance. Zero net external force gives constant total momentum.','p = mv; J = ∫F dt = Δp','Vector quantities; for a system use net external force and total momentum.'],
[10,'Rotation','Two points on the same rigid rotating disk share which quantity?',['Tangential speed at different radii.','Centripetal acceleration at different radii.','Angular velocity.','Arc length traveled at different radii.'],2,'Rigid-body rotation gives all points the same angular velocity. Their tangential speeds depend on radius.','v = rω; aₜ = rα; aᵣ = rω²','For rotation about a fixed axis; r is perpendicular distance from that axis.'],
[11,'Rolling and angular momentum','For pure rolling on a stationary surface, which relation holds?',['vCM = Rω','vCM = ω/R','vCM = 0 always','ω = 0 always'],0,'The no-slip contact constraint makes translational speed of the center equal R times angular speed.','L = r × p; Στext = dL/dt','Use the same reference point and an appropriate inertial origin for the torque–angular momentum relation.'],
[12,'Equilibrium and elasticity','What must hold for a rigid body in static equilibrium?',['Only net force is zero.','Only net torque is zero.','Both net force and net torque are zero.','Its weight is zero.'],2,'Zero net force prevents translational acceleration; zero net torque prevents angular acceleration.','ΣF = 0; Στ = 0; stress = Y × strain','The elastic relation assumes small deformation within the material’s linear elastic range.'],
[13,'Gravitation','If separation between two point masses doubles, their gravitational attraction becomes what fraction?',['One half.','One fourth.','Twice.','Four times.'],1,'The inverse-square law gives Fnew/Fold = 1/2² = 1/4.','F = Gm₁m₂/r²; U = −Gm₁m₂/r','Point masses or spherically symmetric bodies outside their surfaces; potential is zero at infinity.'],
[14,'Fluids','For a static fluid of constant density, how does pressure change with depth?',['It decreases linearly.','It is always zero.','It increases by ρg times the depth increase.','It depends only on container width.'],2,'The weight of the overlying fluid gives Δp = ρgΔh for a constant-density fluid.','p = p₀ + ρgh; FB = ρfluid gVdisplaced','Hydrostatic model with constant density and gravitational acceleration.'],
[15,'Oscillations','In simple harmonic motion, acceleration is proportional to what?',['Displacement in the same direction.','Negative displacement from equilibrium.','Speed only.','Time only.'],1,'The restoring acceleration points toward equilibrium and has magnitude proportional to displacement.','a = −ω²x; T = 2π/ω; ωspring = √(k/m)','Ideal linear spring and negligible damping for the spring formula.'],
[16,'Waves I','Does a traveling wave generally require the medium to travel along with the wave?',['Yes, every particle follows the crest.','No, particles oscillate while the disturbance travels.','Only if frequency is zero.','Only if wavelength is zero.'],1,'A mechanical wave transports a disturbance and energy. Individual medium particles can oscillate about equilibrium.','v = fλ; vstring = √(Tension/μ)','Ideal taut string with small transverse displacement and uniform linear density μ.'],
[17,'Waves II','For a point source radiating uniformly, how does intensity change when distance doubles?',['It doubles.','It halves.','It stays constant.','It becomes one fourth.'],3,'The same power spreads over an area 4πr², so doubling r reduces intensity by four.','I = P/(4πr²); β = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀)','Uniform spherical spreading without absorption; sound level β is in decibels.'],
[18,'Temperature and heat','Thermal equilibrium between two bodies implies equality of what?',['Mass.','Temperature.','Internal energy.','Volume.'],1,'Thermal equilibrium means no net heat transfer caused by a temperature difference.','Q = mcΔT; Qphase = mL','Use mcΔT within one phase with appropriate specific heat; mL describes a phase change.'],
[19,'Kinetic theory','For an ideal gas, average translational kinetic energy per molecule depends on what?',['Absolute temperature.','Container shape only.','Molecular diameter only.','Pressure alone regardless of density.'],0,'The mean translational kinetic energy is 3kBT/2, determined by absolute temperature.','pV = NkBT = nRT; ⟨Ktrans⟩ = 3kBT/2','Ideal-gas model; use kelvins.'],
[20,'Entropy and the second law','For an isolated system, total entropy can do what?',['Only decrease.','Never change.','Increase or remain constant.','Always become zero.'],2,'The second law gives ΔS ≥ 0 for an isolated system, with equality for a reversible process.','ΔS = ∫rev dQ/T; ηCarnot = 1 − TC/TH','Compute entropy along a reversible path; reservoir temperatures must be in kelvins.'],
[21,'Electric charge','Two positive point charges exert what kind of electric force on one another?',['Repulsive.','Attractive.','Zero at every distance.','Tangential to their separation.'],0,'Like charges repel along the line joining the charges; unlike charges attract.','|F| = k|q₁q₂|/r²; k = 1/(4πε₀)','Point charges in vacuum; include directions when adding forces.'],
[22,'Electric fields','How is electric field related to the force on a positive test charge?',['E = qF','E = F/q','E = F²/q','E = q/F'],1,'Electric field is force per unit positive test charge and points in the direction of that force.','E = F/q; Epoint = kq r̂/r²','Test-charge limit; superpose vector fields from multiple charges.'],
[23,'Gauss’s law','Electric flux through a closed surface depends on which charge?',['Every charge outside the surface.','Net enclosed charge.','Only the largest charge nearby.','Only charges exactly on the surface.'],1,'Gauss’s law relates net outward flux to net charge enclosed, even though outside charges may affect the local field.','∮ E · dA = Qenclosed/ε₀','Closed surface with outward area vectors; symmetry is needed to easily solve for E.'],
[24,'Electric potential','Moving a charge q through potential difference ΔV changes its potential energy by what?',['q/ΔV','ΔV/q','qΔV','q²ΔV²'],2,'Potential difference is potential-energy change per unit charge, so ΔU = qΔV.','ΔU = qΔV; E = −∇V','Electrostatics; electric potential is scalar and its reference zero is arbitrary.'],
[25,'Capacitance','For a fixed ideal capacitor, doubling applied voltage does what to stored charge magnitude?',['Halves it.','Doubles it.','Leaves it unchanged.','Makes it zero.'],1,'Q = CV and C is fixed in this ideal model, so charge is proportional to voltage.','C = Q/V; U = ½CV²','Linear capacitor with fixed geometry and dielectric response.'],
[26,'Current and resistance','Electric current is the rate of change of which quantity through a cross-section?',['Energy.','Voltage.','Charge.','Resistance.'],2,'Current is charge flow per unit time, I = dQ/dt.','I = dQ/dt; V = IR; R = ρL/A','V = IR assumes ohmic behavior at the stated physical conditions.'],
[27,'Circuits','Kirchhoff’s junction rule follows from conservation of what?',['Charge.','Temperature.','Resistance.','Capacitance.'],0,'Charge does not accumulate at an ideal steady-state junction, so total incoming current equals outgoing current.','Σjunction I = 0; Σloop ΔV = 0','Use consistent signs; the simple loop rule assumes no unaccounted changing magnetic flux.'],
[28,'Magnetic fields','A charge moving parallel to a magnetic field experiences what magnetic force?',['qvB in the direction of motion.','Zero.','qvB opposite its motion.','qB independent of speed.'],1,'The magnetic force qv × B is zero for parallel or antiparallel velocity and field.','F = q(v × B); |F| = |q|vB sin θ','This is the magnetic contribution; add qE if an electric field is also present.'],
[29,'Fields due to currents','Around a long straight wire, the magnetic field direction is what?',['Radially inward.','Radially outward.','Along the wire everywhere.','Tangent to circles around the wire.'],3,'The right-hand rule gives circular field lines around the current direction.','B = μ₀I/(2πr)','Long straight wire in vacuum, at perpendicular distance r outside the wire.'],
[30,'Induction','Lenz’s law fixes an induced current’s direction so that its magnetic effect does what?',['Opposes the change in magnetic flux.','Always increases the original flux.','Always points north.','Eliminates resistance.'],0,'The induced effect opposes the change in flux that produces it, consistent with energy conservation.','ε = −N dΦB/dt; ΦB = ∫B · dA','N identical coil turns; orient the loop normal and positive circulation consistently.'],
[31,'Electromagnetic oscillations','An ideal LC circuit repeatedly exchanges energy between which stores?',['Heat and sound.','Electric field and magnetic field.','Chemical and gravitational.','Rest mass and friction.'],1,'Energy oscillates between the capacitor’s electric field and the inductor’s magnetic field.','ω₀ = 1/√(LC); UL = ½LI²','Ideal LC oscillation neglects resistance and energy loss.'],
[32,'Maxwell’s equations and magnetism','What does zero net magnetic flux through a closed surface express?',['Magnetic fields never exist.','Magnetic poles occur only as isolated north poles.','No isolated magnetic monopoles in classical Maxwell theory.','Electric charge is always zero.'],2,'Gauss’s law for magnetism gives zero net flux through a closed surface; magnetic field lines form continuous loops.','∮B · dA = 0; ∮B · dl = μ₀Ienclosed + μ₀ε₀ dΦE/dt','Integral Maxwell equations in vacuum with consistently oriented surfaces and paths.'],
[33,'Electromagnetic waves','In a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum, E and B are oriented how?',['Parallel to each other and propagation.','Perpendicular to each other and propagation.','Both parallel only to propagation.','Both always zero.'],1,'A vacuum plane electromagnetic wave is transverse: E, B, and propagation form mutually perpendicular directions.','c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀); E₀ = cB₀','Plane traveling electromagnetic wave in vacuum.'],
[34,'Images','For a plane mirror, the image lies where?',['At the mirror surface.','Twice the object distance in front.','The same distance behind as the object is in front.','At infinity for every object.'],2,'Reflected rays appear to come from a virtual image equally far behind the plane mirror.','1/f = 1/p + 1/i; m = −i/p','Paraxial thin-lens or spherical-mirror equation with the book’s corresponding sign convention; plane mirror i = −p.'],
[35,'Interference','Two coherent waves arriving in phase tend to produce what?',['Constructive interference.','Destructive interference only.','No superposition.','Zero intensity regardless of amplitude.'],0,'In-phase displacements add. Equal in-phase waves double amplitude and give four times one wave’s intensity.','Double slit: d sin θ = mλ','Bright fringes for coherent, in-phase illumination of narrow slits; m is an integer.'],
[36,'Diffraction','Making a single slit narrower generally does what to its central diffraction maximum?',['Makes it narrower.','Makes it wider.','Leaves its width unchanged.','Eliminates wavelength dependence.'],1,'The first minimum satisfies a sin θ = λ, so decreasing slit width a increases the angular spread.','Single slit: a sin θ = mλ, m = ±1, ±2, …','Fraunhofer single-slit minima; the formula does not describe the central maximum at m = 0.'],
[37,'Relativity','Which speed is the same for all inertial observers in special relativity?',['Any object’s speed.','A sound wave’s speed in air.','Light’s speed in vacuum.','A moving clock’s hand speed.'],2,'The invariance of vacuum light speed is a postulate of special relativity.','γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²); Δt = γΔτ','Δτ is proper time between events occurring at one position in the clock’s rest frame.'],
[38,'Photons and matter waves','Increasing a photon’s frequency increases what?',['Its energy.','Its rest mass.','Its vacuum speed.','Its wavelength.'],0,'Photon energy is hf. Its vacuum speed stays c and its wavelength decreases as frequency increases.','E = hf = hc/λ; λdeBroglie = h/p','For a photon use its vacuum wavelength; the de Broglie relation uses particle momentum.'],
[39,'More about matter waves','What is the physical meaning of |ψ|² for a normalized one-particle wavefunction?',['Electric charge itself.','Probability density.','Exact classical trajectory.','Particle speed.'],1,'The squared magnitude gives probability per unit configuration-space volume. Its full-space integral is one.','∫|ψ|² dV = 1; En = n²h²/(8mL²)','Energy formula for a nonrelativistic particle in a one-dimensional infinite well of width L, n = 1,2,…'],
[40,'All about atoms','In the simple hydrogen model, atomic energy levels are what?',['Continuous at all bound energies.','Quantized.','Always positive for bound states.','Independent of principal quantum number.'],1,'Bound hydrogen states occupy discrete energies labeled by integer principal quantum number n.','En ≈ −13.6 eV/n²','Nonrelativistic hydrogen approximation, neglecting fine structure and reduced-mass corrections.'],
[41,'Conduction of electricity in solids','What is the defining band feature of an intrinsic semiconductor?',['A small gap between valence and conduction bands.','No allowed electron states.','An infinitely large band gap.','A perfectly filled conduction band at all temperatures.'],0,'A relatively small band gap permits thermally excited electrons in the conduction band and holes in the valence band.','J = σE; σ = neμe + nhμh','Simple electron-and-hole transport model with carrier densities n and mobilities μ.'],
[42,'Nuclear physics','After one half-life, what fraction of a large radioactive sample’s parent nuclei remains on average?',['One fourth.','One half.','Two thirds.','All of it.'],1,'Half-life is the time for the expected parent-nucleus population to decrease by a factor of two.','N(t) = N₀e⁻λt; T½ = ln 2/λ; A = λN','Constant decay probability; predictions describe the statistical population.'],
[43,'Energy from the nucleus','Energy released in a nuclear reaction corresponds to what?',['An increase in total rest mass only.','A decrease in total rest mass.','A violation of energy conservation.','A change in electric charge conservation.'],1,'A lower final rest mass leaves energy available as particle kinetic energy or radiation while total energy is conserved.','Q = (minitial − mfinal)c²','Use consistent nuclear or atomic mass accounting and include all reaction products.'],
[44,'Quarks, leptons, and the Big Bang','In the quark model, a proton contains which valence quarks?',['uud','udd','uuu','ddd'],0,'Two up quarks and one down quark have total charge 2/3 + 2/3 − 1/3 = +1 in elementary-charge units.','Qup = +(2/3)e; Qdown = −(1/3)e','These are quark electric charges; composite-particle charge is the sum of constituent charges.']
]};

// Added textbook chapter summaries.
window.STUDY_DATA["chemistry"] = [[1,"Essential Ideas","Which observation provides evidence that a chemical change has occurred?",["A new substance with different chemical properties forms.","A sample is cut into smaller pieces.","A liquid changes shape to fit its container.","A solid is measured on a balance."],0,"A chemical change changes chemical identity. Cutting, changing container shape, and measuring mass can occur without forming a new substance.","ρ = m/V","ρ is density, m is sample mass, and V is its volume. Use compatible units: SI density is kg/m³; laboratory densities often use g/mL. The measured sample and its temperature must match the stated density."],[2,"Atoms, Molecules, and Ions","What distinguishes two isotopes of the same element?",["They have different numbers of protons.","They have different numbers of neutrons.","They must have opposite electrical charges.","They have different atomic numbers."],1,"Isotopes share the same atomic number and therefore the same proton count, but differ in neutron count and mass number. Changing electrons instead creates ions.","A = Z + N; q/e = Z − Nₑ","A is mass number, Z is proton count (atomic number), N is neutron count, and Nₑ is electron count. q is the atom or ion charge; e is the positive elementary charge. These are particle counts, so A is not the element’s average atomic mass."],[3,"Electronic Structure and Periodic Properties of Elements","For a photon traveling in vacuum, what happens to its energy when its wavelength increases?",["Its energy increases because the wave is longer.","Its energy stays fixed for every photon.","Its energy decreases because energy is inversely proportional to wavelength.","Its energy becomes negative."],2,"Photon energy is E = hc/λ. With h and c fixed, a larger wavelength gives a smaller positive energy and a lower frequency.","E = hν = hc/λ","E is photon energy in J, h = 6.62607015 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s, ν is frequency in s⁻¹, c = 2.99792458 × 10⁸ m/s, and λ is vacuum wavelength in m. Use vacuum c; convert nm to m before calculating."],[4,"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Geometry","A central atom has four electron domains: two bonds and two lone pairs. What molecular shape does VSEPR predict?",["Linear.","Trigonal planar.","Tetrahedral molecular shape.","Bent molecular shape."],3,"Four electron domains have a tetrahedral electron-domain arrangement. Molecular shape describes only the bonded atoms, so two bonds plus two lone pairs give a bent shape.","FC = V − L − B/2","FC is formal charge in elementary-charge units, V is the neutral atom’s valence-electron count, L is its nonbonding-electron count, and B is its bonding-electron count. Count every lone-pair electron and split bonding electrons equally. Formal charge is bookkeeping, not a measured partial charge."],[5,"Advanced Theories of Bonding","According to molecular orbital theory, what makes a molecule paramagnetic?",["At least one occupied molecular orbital contains an unpaired electron.","Every electron is paired in an occupied orbital.","The molecule has no antibonding orbitals.","The molecule’s bond order must equal zero."],0,"Unpaired electrons make a species paramagnetic. A species whose electrons are all paired is diamagnetic; bonding and antibonding occupation alone does not settle this without examining electron pairing.","Bond order = (N_bonding − N_antibonding)/2","N_bonding and N_antibonding count electrons in bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals in the chosen MO diagram. Fill the appropriate diagram using Aufbau, Pauli, and Hund rules. This MO bond order is a model of net bonding; zero does not predict a stable ordinary covalent bond."],[6,"Composition of Substances and Solutions","When calculating molarity, which volume belongs in the denominator?",["The volume of solute alone.","The final volume of the complete solution.","The solvent volume before the solute is added in every case.","The volume of the measuring flask’s glass."],1,"Molarity is moles of solute divided by total solution volume. Adding solute can change volume, so the solvent’s original volume is not automatically the final solution volume.","c = n_solute/V_solution; n = m/M_m","c is molarity in mol/L, n is amount in mol, V_solution is final solution volume in L, m is solute mass in g, and M_m is molar mass in g/mol. Convert mL to L. Molarity depends on temperature because solution volume can change."],[7,"Stoichiometry of Chemical Reactions","What do the coefficients in a balanced chemical equation tell you for a mole-to-mole conversion?",["The masses of reactants must be equal.","The molar masses of reactants and products are equal.","The relative amounts in moles that react or form.","The reaction must go completely in both directions at once."],2,"Balanced coefficients give stoichiometric mole ratios, not mass ratios. To convert a mass, first use molar mass to find moles, then apply the balanced coefficient ratio.","n_B = (b/a)n_A","a and b are the balanced coefficients of consumed A and formed B; n_A is the amount of A that actually reacts, and n_B is the stoichiometric amount of B formed. For theoretical yield, use the limiting reactant and assume the stated reaction proceeds completely without side reactions. Excess reactant does not determine the yield."],[8,"Gases","Why must temperature be expressed in kelvin in the ideal gas law?",["Kelvin and Celsius have identical zero points.","Kelvin values remove the need to choose pressure units.","The gas constant is only defined for temperatures below freezing.","The law uses absolute temperature, whose zero represents the absolute thermal scale."],3,"Gas-law proportionalities use absolute temperature. Celsius has an arbitrary offset, so doubling a Celsius reading does not double absolute temperature. Convert using T(K) = T(°C) + 273.15.","PV = nRT","P is absolute pressure, V is gas volume, n is amount in mol, T is temperature in K, and R = 8.314462618 J/(mol·K) for P in Pa and V in m³. Alternatively use R = 0.082057 L·atm/(mol·K) with L and atm. The ideal approximation works best at relatively low pressure and high temperature, away from condensation."],[9,"Thermochemistry","If a reaction releases heat to its surroundings at constant pressure, what is the sign of the reaction’s enthalpy change?",["Negative: the reacting system loses heat.","Positive: the surroundings become warmer.","Always zero because energy is conserved.","Its sign is determined only by the reaction speed."],0,"An exothermic reaction transfers heat out of the reacting system, so its ΔH is negative at constant pressure. The surroundings gain heat; system and surroundings have opposite heat-transfer signs.","q = mcΔT; q_p = ΔH","q is heat transferred into the chosen sample, m is mass, c is specific heat capacity, and ΔT = T_final − T_initial. With m in g and c in J/(g·K), q is in J. Treat c as constant over the range and exclude phase changes. q_p = ΔH applies at constant pressure with only pressure–volume work; distinguish the reaction system from calorimeter surroundings."],[10,"Liquids and Solids","Why does a liquid boil when its vapor pressure equals the external pressure?",["All intermolecular attractions have permanently disappeared.","Vapor bubbles can persist and grow throughout the liquid.","Its molecules stop moving.","The liquid must be at its critical temperature."],1,"At the boiling point, vapor pressure balances the external pressure, allowing vapor bubbles to exist inside the liquid. Lowering external pressure lowers the boiling temperature.","ln(P₂/P₁) = −(ΔH_vap/R)(1/T₂ − 1/T₁)","P₁ and P₂ are equilibrium vapor pressures of the same substance; T₁ and T₂ are absolute temperatures in K. ΔH_vap is molar enthalpy of vaporization in J/mol and R = 8.314462618 J/(mol·K). ln is the natural logarithm. This integrated Clausius–Clapeyron approximation assumes ideal vapor, negligible liquid volume, and nearly constant ΔH_vap over the temperature interval."],[11,"Solutions and Colloids","For ideal dilute solutions with the same solvent, what primarily determines the size of a colligative effect?",["The color of the dissolved solute.","The shape of the container.","The number of dissolved solute particles relative to the solvent amount.","Only the mass of one solute molecule."],2,"Colligative properties depend on the amount of dissolved particles rather than their chemical identity in the ideal dilute limit. Dissociation can increase particle count, so equal formula-unit concentrations need not give equal effects.","ΔT_f = iK_f b","ΔT_f = T_freezing,pure − T_freezing,solution is a positive freezing-point depression, i is the effective van ’t Hoff factor, K_f is the solvent’s freezing-point constant in K·kg/mol, and b is solute molality in mol/kg of solvent. Use solvent mass, not solution volume. This is a dilute-solution approximation; ion interactions can make i differ from the ideal dissociation count."],[12,"Thermodynamics","At fixed temperature and pressure, what does ΔG < 0 tell you about the stated process?",["It must happen instantaneously.","It must release heat in every case.","It cannot proceed spontaneously.","It is thermodynamically spontaneous in the stated direction under those conditions."],3,"A negative Gibbs free energy change indicates a spontaneous direction at fixed temperature and pressure. It does not predict reaction speed; an activation barrier can make a spontaneous reaction very slow.","ΔG = ΔH − TΔS","ΔG is Gibbs free energy change, ΔH is enthalpy change, ΔS is entropy change, and T is absolute temperature in K. Use consistent energy units: convert ΔS from J/(mol·K) if ΔH is in kJ/mol. The simple finite-change expression assumes the initial and final states are at the same temperature; the spontaneity criterion applies at fixed T and P with only pressure–volume work."],[13,"Fundamental Equilibrium Concepts","What is true when a reversible chemical reaction reaches dynamic equilibrium?",["The forward and reverse reaction rates are equal, though reactions continue.","Both reactions stop permanently.","Every reactant concentration equals every product concentration.","The equilibrium constant becomes zero."],0,"Dynamic equilibrium means equal forward and reverse rates. Concentrations remain constant, but their values need not be equal, and molecular-scale reactions continue.","K_c = [C]^c[D]^d/([A]^a[B]^b)","For aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD, a, b, c, d are balanced stoichiometric coefficients and bracketed terms are equilibrium molar concentrations. Omit pure solids and pure liquids. K_c belongs to the written reaction at a specified temperature; reverse the reaction to obtain its reciprocal. Concentration expressions are the introductory approximation to dimensionless activity-based equilibrium constants."],[14,"Acid-Base Equilibria","Which statement describes a Brønsted–Lowry conjugate acid–base pair?",["The two species differ by one electron.","The two species differ by one proton, H⁺.","They must have equal electrical charges.","They must both contain hydroxide ions."],1,"An acid becomes its conjugate base by donating one proton, and a base becomes its conjugate acid by accepting one proton. An acid–base conjugate pair differs by exactly H⁺, not by an electron.","pH = −log₁₀ a(H₃O⁺) ≈ −log₁₀([H₃O⁺]/(1 mol/L))","a(H₃O⁺) is the dimensionless hydronium activity. In a sufficiently dilute aqueous solution, approximate it by molar concentration divided by the standard concentration 1 mol/L. log₁₀ is base ten. Neutrality means [H₃O⁺] = [OH⁻]; neutral pH is 7.00 at 25 °C and changes when the water ionization constant changes with temperature."],[15,"Equilibria of Other Reaction Classes","For a dilute AgCl solution at a fixed temperature, what does Q_sp > K_sp predict?",["More AgCl must dissolve until all solid disappears.","No phase change is possible.","AgCl precipitation is thermodynamically favored until equilibrium is restored.","The value of K_sp permanently increases."],2,"Q_sp is the current ion product. If it exceeds K_sp, the solution is supersaturated with respect to AgCl and precipitation lowers dissolved ion concentrations toward equilibrium. K_sp stays fixed at the same temperature.","AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq); K_sp ≈ [Ag⁺][Cl⁻]","The dissolution has a 1:1 ion ratio. Brackets denote equilibrium molar concentrations in the dilute approximation; the exact thermodynamic expression uses dimensionless activities. Pure solid AgCl is omitted because its activity is one. Q_sp uses current concentrations and K_sp uses equilibrium values at the given temperature; do not use K_sp = s² when a common ion or other equilibrium changes the ion concentrations."],[16,"Electrochemistry","Where does oxidation occur in an electrochemical cell?",["At the cathode in every cell.","Only inside the salt bridge.","At whichever electrode has the larger physical area.","At the anode in both galvanic and electrolytic cells."],3,"Oxidation is electron loss and always occurs at the anode; reduction occurs at the cathode. Electrode signs differ between galvanic and electrolytic cells, so naming the process is more reliable than memorizing a sign alone.","E°_cell = E°_cathode − E°_anode; ΔG° = −nFE°_cell","Use standard reduction potentials for both electrodes, subtracting anode from cathode. E° is in V; n is moles of electrons transferred per mole of the balanced cell reaction, and F = 96485.33212 C/mol. ΔG° is in J/mol of reaction. Standard conditions use unit activities and the table’s temperature. Never multiply electrode potentials by balancing coefficients; only n scales when the reaction is multiplied."],[17,"Kinetics","Can the reaction orders in a general experimentally measured rate law always be read from the overall balanced equation?",["No; overall reaction orders generally must be determined experimentally.","Yes; every exponent always equals its balanced coefficient.","Yes; every reactant always has first order.","No; reaction orders can never be determined."],0,"The overall balanced equation gives stoichiometry, but generally does not specify the rate law. Orders come from experiment unless a known elementary step justifies using its reactant coefficients.","rate = k[A]^m[B]^n","k is the rate constant at the specified temperature, [A] and [B] are reactant molar concentrations, and m and n are experimental reaction orders. For rate in mol/(L·s), units of k are (mol/L)^(1−m−n)/s. Orders may be zero, fractional, or negative. Do not equate orders with overall stoichiometric coefficients without a justified elementary-step model."],[18,"Representative Metals, Metalloids, and Nonmetals","When a carbonate ion reacts with enough aqueous acid, which gas is formed?",["Hydrogen, H₂.","Carbon dioxide, CO₂.","Nitrogen, N₂.","Chlorine, Cl₂."],1,"Acid protonates carbonate to form carbonic acid, which yields water and carbon dioxide. The net ionic equation consumes two H⁺ per carbonate ion and conserves both atoms and charge.","CO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)","This is a balanced net ionic reaction for carbonate under acidic aqueous conditions; spectator ions are omitted. One mole of carbonate consumes two moles of H⁺ and can form one mole of CO₂ with sufficient acid. CO₃²⁻ has −2 charge, so the total charge is zero on both sides. Bicarbonate, HCO₃⁻, instead requires one H⁺ per ion."],[19,"Transition Metals and Coordination Chemistry","A metal is bonded to six monodentate NH₃ ligands. What is its coordination number?",["Three because NH₃ contains three hydrogens.","Zero because NH₃ is electrically neutral.","Six because six ligand donor atoms bond to the metal.","The same number as the metal’s oxidation state in every case."],2,"Coordination number counts donor atoms directly bonded to the central metal. Each monodentate NH₃ supplies one donor nitrogen, so six NH₃ ligands provide six donor atoms. Coordination number and oxidation state are different quantities.","q_complex = z_metal + Σq_ligands","q_complex is the complex ion’s net charge, z_metal is the metal oxidation state, and Σq_ligands is the sum of all ligand charges, counting each occurrence. Use elementary-charge units. Neutral NH₃ and H₂O each contribute zero; Cl⁻ contributes −1. Charge bookkeeping determines oxidation state, not coordination number or high-spin versus low-spin electron arrangement."],[20,"Nuclear Chemistry","For a fixed radionuclide undergoing ordinary first-order radioactive decay, what happens to its half-life when the initial sample contains more nuclei?",["It doubles whenever the sample doubles.","It becomes zero for large samples.","It is determined by the sample’s chemical color.","It remains the same; only the initial activity increases."],3,"The decay constant and half-life are properties of the radionuclide under ordinary conditions. A larger number of undecayed nuclei gives higher initial activity, but the same fraction decays during one half-life.","N(t) = N₀e^(−λt); t₁/₂ = ln(2)/λ","N(t) is the number of undecayed parent nuclei at time t, N₀ is its initial value, λ is the positive decay constant, and ln is the natural logarithm. Use the same time units for t and 1/λ. This single-parent exponential assumes no replenishment of that parent and no mixture of different decay constants; daughter ingrowth needs additional equations."],[21,"Organic Chemistry","Which structural feature distinguishes an alkene from an alkane?",["At least one carbon–carbon double bond.","An oxygen atom in every molecule.","A nitrogen atom in every molecule.","Only carbon–carbon single bonds and no multiple bonds."],0,"An alkene contains a carbon–carbon double bond. Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons with only single bonds. Carbon rings and oxygen- or nitrogen-containing functional groups require additional structural classification.","CₙH₂ₙ₊₂","This molecular formula applies to a neutral acyclic saturated hydrocarbon (an alkane) with n ≥ 1 carbon atoms and no heteroatoms. It does not apply unchanged to rings or multiple bonds: one ring or one double bond reduces hydrogen count by two relative to the acyclic alkane. A formula alone does not identify connectivity."]];
window.STUDY_DATA["statics"] = [[1,"General principles","Which quantity is a force rather than a mass?",["Weight in newtons.","Mass in kilograms.","Density in kilograms per cubic meter.","Time in seconds."],0,"Weight is the gravitational force W = mg. Mass is an inertial property; its SI unit is kg, while force uses N = kg·m/s².","W = mg; 1 kN = 1000 N","W: weight (N); m: mass (kg); g: local gravity (m/s²). Use consistent units before calculating. Near Earth's surface, use g ≈ 9.81 m/s² unless the problem specifies another value."],[2,"Force vectors","A force F makes angle θ above the positive x axis. Which is its x component?",["F cos θ.","F sin θ.","F tan θ.","F / sin θ."],0,"The x projection is adjacent to the angle measured from +x, so use cosine. The y projection uses sine. Component signs come from the direction, not from magnitude alone.","Fₓ = F cos θ; Fᵧ = F sin θ; |F| = √(Fₓ² + Fᵧ² + F𝓏²)","F: force magnitude (N); θ: angle measured from +x; Fₓ, Fᵧ, F𝓏: signed components (N). For a line direction, use F = F(r/|r|). A projection onto a unit direction u is F·u."],[3,"Equilibrium of a particle","What condition must a particle at rest satisfy?",["The vector sum of all its external forces is zero.","Every individual force must be zero.","Only its horizontal forces need to balance.","Its weight must equal zero."],0,"A particle cannot accelerate when its net external force is zero. Nonzero forces may balance; resolve the same free-body diagram along every independent axis.","ΣF = 0 ⇒ ΣFₓ = 0, ΣFᵧ = 0, ΣF𝓏 = 0","Each sum includes signed external force components on one isolated particle. Draw a free-body diagram first; cable tension pulls along the cable away from the particle. Do not add the forces the particle exerts on its surroundings."],[4,"Force system resultants","What distance belongs in the scalar moment magnitude Fd?",["The perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force's line of action.","Any distance from the pivot to the object.","The length of the force arrow on the drawing.","The distance between the endpoints of the beam only."],0,"Moment is force multiplied by its perpendicular lever arm. A force whose line of action passes through the pivot has zero moment there even if its application point is far away.","Mₒ = r × F; M𝓏 = xFᵧ − yFₓ; |M| = Fd⊥","r: position from the moment center to a point on the force line (m); F: force (N); M: moment (N·m). In 2D, choose counterclockwise positive unless stated otherwise. A distributed load resultant equals the area under its load-intensity diagram and acts at that area's centroid."],[5,"Equilibrium of a rigid body","Which equations are needed for a general planar rigid body in equilibrium?",["ΣFₓ = 0, ΣFᵧ = 0, and ΣMₒ = 0.","Only ΣFₓ = 0.","Only ΣFᵧ = 0.","Only ΣMₒ = 0."],0,"Both translation and rotation must balance. Choose the moment center at a support to eliminate reactions acting there and simplify the equations.","ΣFₓ = 0; ΣFᵧ = 0; ΣMₒ = 0","Use one free-body diagram and consistent sign conventions. A planar pin supplies two force components; a frictionless roller supplies one normal reaction; a fixed support supplies two force components and a reaction moment. Equilibrium equations alone require a statically determinate support arrangement."],[6,"Structural analysis","In an ideal pin-connected truss, where do external loads act for the two-force-member model?",["At the joints.","Anywhere along each member with no change to the model.","Only at each member's midpoint.","Only perpendicular to every member."],0,"With ideal pins and joint-applied loads, a member has only two end forces, which must be equal, opposite, and collinear. Members therefore carry axial tension or compression.","At each joint: ΣFₓ = 0 and ΣFᵧ = 0","Assume unknown member forces pull away from the joint (tension); a negative result indicates compression. For an unloaded joint with two collinear members and one non-collinear member, the non-collinear member has zero force. Do not apply the rule where a load or support reaction acts."],[7,"Internal forces","Under the convention dM/dx = V, what does the signed area under a shear diagram give?",["The change in bending moment over that interval.","The absolute bending moment without an initial value.","The area moment of inertia of the cross section.","The axial force in every case."],0,"Integrating shear gives the moment change. Add the moment at the interval start to obtain the moment at its end.","dV/dx = −w; dM/dx = V; ΔV = −∫w dx; ΔM = ∫V dx","x: position along the beam (m); w: downward load intensity (N/m); V: signed shear (N); M: signed bending moment (N·m). These derivative signs depend on the stated beam convention. At a cut, draw internal axial force, shear, and moment; use equilibrium on one segment."],[8,"Friction","When is the actual static friction magnitude equal to μₛN?",["At impending sliding.","At every instant a body is at rest.","Only when the normal force is zero.","Whenever the object has mass."],0,"Static friction adjusts to the required balance while |F| ≤ μₛN. Equality occurs at the sliding threshold; using equality too early can give a false equilibrium result.","|Fstatic| ≤ μₛN; Fmax = μₛN; Fkinetic = μₖN","N: contact normal force (N); μₛ and μₖ: dimensionless coefficients. Friction opposes actual or impending relative motion at the contact. For an ideal flexible belt at impending slip, Ttight/Tslack = exp(μₛβ), with wrap angle β in radians."],[9,"Center of gravity and centroid","For two plate regions of unequal area, how is the combined centroid found?",["Use an area-weighted average of their centroid coordinates.","Use the unweighted mean of the two coordinates.","Use only the centroid of the larger region.","Multiply the two centroid coordinates."],0,"Each first moment is area times coordinate. Divide the sum of first moments by total area. Treat holes as negative areas and use one common origin.","x̄ = ΣAᵢxᵢ / ΣAᵢ; ȳ = ΣAᵢyᵢ / ΣAᵢ","Aᵢ: signed component area (m²); xᵢ and yᵢ: that area's centroid coordinates (m). Use mass weights for a mass centroid when density is nonuniform. For pressure on a submerged plane area: F = ρg h̄ A; h̄ is centroid depth, not center-of-pressure depth."],[10,"Moments of inertia","Which equation shifts an area moment from a centroidal axis to a parallel axis distance d away?",["I = Ic + Ad².","I = Ic − Ad².","I = Ic + Ad.","I = mgh."],0,"The parallel-axis theorem adds area times distance squared. Ic must refer to the parallel centroidal axis; the shift always increases or preserves area inertia.","I = Ic + Ad²; k = √(I/A); Ix,rectangle = bh³/12","Area inertia I has units m⁴; A has m²; d and gyration radius k have m. The rectangle formula uses a horizontal centroidal axis with vertical dimension h. Mass inertia is a different quantity: I = ∫r²dm (kg·m²), with mass parallel-axis theorem I = IG + md²."],[11,"Virtual work","For a smooth one-coordinate conservative system, which condition indicates stable equilibrium at a stationary point?",["The potential has a strict local minimum.","The potential has a strict local maximum.","The potential is positive at that point.","The coordinate happens to equal zero."],0,"Equilibrium requires V′ = 0. A strict potential minimum is stable because small displacements raise potential; V″ > 0 is a sufficient local test. The sign of V itself does not determine stability.","δU = ΣF·δr + ΣM δθ = 0; V′(q) = 0 at equilibrium","Virtual displacements must satisfy the system's constraints; ideal constraint forces do no virtual work. A couple's rotation δθ is in radians. For a smooth one-coordinate conservative system, V″ > 0 gives stable and V″ < 0 unstable equilibrium; V″ = 0 is inconclusive and requires further analysis."]];
window.STUDY_DATA["dynamics"] = [[12,"Particle kinematics","A particle moves at constant nonzero speed around a circle. Is its acceleration zero?",["Yes: speed is constant.","No: velocity direction changes.","Yes: position is constant.","Only if its mass is positive."],1,"Velocity includes direction. Curved motion has inward normal acceleration even at constant speed.","aₙ = v²/ρ; aₜ = dv/dt","ρ is the local radius of curvature; tangential acceleration describes changing speed."],[13,"Particle force and acceleration","Which equation relates resultant external force to acceleration in an inertial frame?",["ΣF = mv","ΣF = ma","ΣF = m/a","ΣF = 0 in every moving system"],1,"Resultant force sets acceleration, not velocity. A moving particle can have zero net force.","ΣF = ma; ΣFₙ = mv²/ρ","Apply along defined axes; signs depend on the chosen positive directions."],[14,"Particle work and energy","For constant mass, net work equals which change?",["Velocity","Momentum","Kinetic energy","Mass"],2,"Work accumulates force along displacement and changes kinetic energy.","T₁ + U₁→₂ = T₂; T = ½mv²","Include all net work, or use potential energy consistently without double counting."],[15,"Particle impulse and momentum","What changes a system’s total linear momentum?",["Internal forces alone","Net external impulse","Elapsed time alone","Kinetic energy alone"],1,"Internal forces cancel pairwise; external force integrated over time changes system momentum.","p₂ − p₁ = ∫ΣF_ext dt","Momentum conservation requires negligible net external impulse in the direction considered."],[16,"Planar rigid-body kinematics","For a wheel rolling without slip on a stationary floor, which point has zero instantaneous velocity?",["The top point","The center","The contact point","Every point"],2,"Translation and rotation cancel at the contact point; its acceleration need not be zero.","v_B = v_A + ω × r_B/A","Relative velocity applies to points fixed on the same rigid body; no-slip gives v_G = ωR."],[17,"Planar rigid-body force and acceleration","For a uniform disk about its fixed central axis, which relation determines angular acceleration?",["ΣM = Iα","ΣM = Iω","ΣM = mα","ΣM = I/α"],0,"The net external moment about the fixed axis equals mass rotational inertia times angular acceleration.","ΣF = ma_G; ΣM_G = I_Gα","For planar rigid-body motion, the center equation and rotational equation are used together."],[18,"Planar rigid-body work and energy","Which terms belong in a freely moving rigid body’s planar kinetic energy?",["Only center translation","Only rotation","Center translation plus rotation about the center","Potential energy only"],2,"Center motion and relative rotation contribute separately.","T = ½mv_G² + ½I_Gω²","I_G is the mass moment of inertia about the center axis perpendicular to the plane."],[19,"Planar rigid-body impulse and momentum","An off-center impulse on a free body initially at rest can cause what?",["Translation only in every case","Rotation only in every case","Both translation and rotation","No motion"],2,"The impulse changes center linear momentum, and its moment changes angular momentum about the center.","m(v_G₂ − v_G₁) = J; I_G(ω₂ − ω₁) = J_M,G","Use the full external impulse and its moment about the center."],[20,"Three-dimensional rigid-body kinematics","For a body-fixed point on a body rotating about a fixed origin, which velocity relation applies?",["v = ω × r","v = ω · r","v = α × r","v = r/ω"],0,"The cross product produces the tangential velocity vector with the proper direction.","v = ω × r; a = α × r + ω × (ω × r)","Point must be fixed in the body; moving relative points require transport and relative-motion terms."],[21,"Three-dimensional rigid-body kinetics","In principal axes, how is the x component of angular momentum found?",["Hₓ = Iₓωₓ","Hₓ = Iᵧωₓ","Hₓ = mωₓ","Hₓ = Iₓ/ωₓ"],0,"The inertia tensor is diagonal along principal axes, so matching inertia and angular velocity components multiply.","H_G = I_Gω; T_rot = ½ω · H_G","Angular momentum need not be parallel to angular velocity unless the relevant inertias match or motion is along a principal axis."],[22,"Vibrations","For a linear spring-mass system, increasing stiffness while keeping mass fixed does what to natural angular frequency?",["Decreases it","Increases it","Leaves it unchanged","Makes it zero"],1,"ωₙ = √(k/m), so greater stiffness increases natural frequency.","ωₙ = √(k/m); ζ = c/(2√(km)); ω_d = ωₙ√(1 − ζ²)","The damped-frequency relation requires the underdamped range 0 ≤ ζ < 1."]];
